**Defect: `clone()` leaks one function object per piece.**

```cpp
c->addPiece(*piece(i).clone());
```

`piece(i).clone()` allocates a new `gsFunction<T>` on the heap. Then `addPiece` clones it again:

```cpp
void addPiece(const gsFunction<T> & func)
{
    m_funcs.push_back( func.clone() );
}
```

The second copy is stored in `m_funcs` and freed by the destructor. The first copy is only used as the argument to `addPiece` and is never deleted. So every call to `clone()` leaks `m_funcs.size()` objects.

**Fix:** let `addPiece` do the cloning, since it already makes the deep copy:

```cpp
for (size_t i = 0; i < m_funcs.size(); ++i)
    c->addPiece(piece(i));
```

**Minor note (not a blocker):** when the object was built with `numPieces > 1`, every slot points to the same function. The clone gives each slot its own copy instead. That is semantically fine, but it uses more memory than the original.

**The two changed lines are fine.** Replacing `math::max(1, …)` with `(std::max<mult_t>)(1, …)` is behaviour-neutral. The explicit template argument also removes the int/`mult_t` deduction ambiguity, and the parentheses guard against the Windows `max` macro.

**The function this hunk touches is still wrong, so it shouldn't merge as is.** In the interior-knot loop of `reduceMultiplicity`:

```cpp
const mult_t m = uit.multiplicity();
if ( m > i )
{
    mtmp.push_back( m + mtmp.back() );       // adds the full multiplicity m
    ktmp.insert(ktmp.end(), m - i, *uit);    // but only inserts m - i knots
}
```

The running multiplicity sum (`m_multSum`) adds the old multiplicity `m`, but only `m - i` copies of the knot are written to `m_repKnots`. After the swap, every cumulative entry from the first surviving interior knot onward is too large by `i` per interior knot. The last entry (`bm + mtmp.back()`) then no longer equals `m_repKnots.size()`.

This breaks the class invariant. Anything that relies on `m_multSum`, such as `uiterator::multiplicity()`, `size()` consistency, `uFind`, `degreeReduce` or `greville_into`, will read the wrong positions or go past the end of the array.

**Fix:** use the reduced multiplicity in both places:

```cpp
const mult_t m = uit.multiplicity() - i;
if ( m > 0 )
{
    mtmp.push_back( m + mtmp.back() );
    ktmp.insert(ktmp.end(), m, *uit);
}
```

**Minor issue in the same function:** `ktmp.reserve(uSize());` duplicates the line before it and should be `mtmp.reserve(uSize());`. This only affects performance.

`increaseMultiplicity` checks out. With `r` running to n−1 (non-boundary) or n (boundary), the final `m_multSum.back()` update is correct.

The `<cv>` reader now dereferences the `component` attribute without checking that it exists:

```cpp
const int cIndex = atoi(child->first_attribute("component")->value());
```

`first_attribute` returns `NULL` when the attribute is missing. Every `<cv>` node written before this patch has no `component` attribute, so loading any existing boundary-conditions XML with corner values will dereference a null pointer and crash.

The same function already treats `component` as optional for `<bc>` nodes: it checks for `NULL` and falls back to `-1`, which means "all components". The `<cv>` path should do the same:

```cpp
int cIndex = -1;
if (const gsXmlAttribute * comp = child->first_attribute("component"))
    cIndex = atoi(comp->value());
```

The writer side (adding `component` to `put`) and the reordered `addCornerValue(cornIndex, val, pIndex, uIndex, cIndex)` call are fine. The patch should not be merged until the reader handles files that lack the attribute, so old files keep loading.

The patch only changes comments, so it has no runtime effect. But one new comment is factually wrong, and in a header that is the API's documentation.

**The defect:** the new doc comment on `isRational()` reads:
```cpp
/// Returns false, since all bases that inherit from gsBasis are not rational.
virtual bool isRational() const { return false;}
```
That statement is false. In G+Smo, `gsRationalBasis<SrcT>` derives from `gsBasis<typename SrcT::Scalar_t>` and overrides `isRational()` to return `true`. NURBS bases (`gsTensorNurbsBasis`, etc.) work the same way. So rational bases do inherit from `gsBasis`.

The method is `virtual` precisely because derived bases may be rational. The comment describes the opposite of how the method is meant to be used. A reader could then wrongly assume that any `gsBasis<T>&` is polynomial and skip the `isRational()` check or weight handling.

**Smaller issue:** the `weights()` comments say "Only for compatibility reasons... It returns an empty matrix". This describes only the base-class default, not the virtual interface. Through a `gsBasis&` that refers to a `gsRationalBasis`, `weights()` returns the actual non-empty weights. The comments should say "Default implementation returns an empty matrix; overridden by rational bases." The current wording, together with the old heading "Member functions that need to be redefined in the derived class," is misleading.

**Suggested fix:**
```cpp
/// Returns the weights of a rational basis. The default implementation
/// (non-rational bases) returns an empty matrix; overridden by gsRationalBasis.
...
/// Returns true if the basis is rational. The default implementation returns
/// false; rational bases (e.g. gsRationalBasis) override this to return true.
virtual bool isRational() const { return false;}
```

The inner loop that writes point coordinates uses the wrong bound, which causes an out-of-bounds read.

**The defect:** in `geoMap2vtk`, `n` is the number of patches (`m_exprdata->multiBasis().nBases()`). The inner loop reuses `n` as the coordinate count:

```cpp
for ( index_t i=0; i!=n; ++i)
    dataArray<< pts(i,j) <<" ";
for ( index_t i=n; i<3; ++i)
    dataArray<<"0 ";
```

`pts` has `pts.rows()` rows, which is the geometric dimension (2 or 3). The number of patches has nothing to do with that. The sibling function `expr2vtk` gets this right by using `vals.rows()`.

**Consequences:**
- **More patches than coordinates (`n > pts.rows()`):** `pts(i,j)` reads past the matrix's rows. Eigen does not bounds-check in release builds. The result is either reads from neighbouring heap memory, which get written into the output file, or a crash.
- **Exactly one patch (`n == 1`):** only the x coordinate is written, followed by two zeros. The geometry comes out silently wrong.
- **Two or three patches:** the output happens to be correct only if `n` matches the dimension.
- **More than three patches:** each point gets more than the declared `NumberOfComponents="3"`, so the VTK file is malformed.

The inner loop variable `i` also shadows the outer patch index `i`, which makes this hard to spot.

**Fix:** use `pts.rows()` in both inner loops, and give the inner index a different name:

```cpp
for ( index_t k=0; k!=pts.rows(); ++k)
    dataArray<< pts(k,j) <<" ";
for ( index_t k=pts.rows(); k<3; ++k)
    dataArray<<"0 ";
```

**Minor issues:**
- The doc comment says "@return Vector of strings of all <DataArrays>", but the function returns `<Points>` blocks.
- `vals` is declared but never used.
- The hunk in `writeParaview_impl` changes the indentation of one commented-out line. It is cosmetic noise and should be reverted.
